 2018 that actually  happened in the House of Representatives  the twelve bills not only pass their  committees but they also all passed the  House of Representatives  however the Republicans attached so many  non-essential ideological measures to  those bills but they were basically dead  on arrival in the Senate where some  Democratic votes are  needed because the Republicans don't  have a veto-proof majority and so when  the deadline came last year to fund the  government for this year on September  30th Congress had completed exactly zero  of the 12 funding laws which led to a  parade of continuing resolutions now  continuing resolutions are funding laws  that are simply and thoughtlessly  extending the funding levels from the  previous year and when federal agencies  have to operate under a continuing  resolution they're not able to start any  new projects or do any long-term  planning and so the agencies hate them  115 Congress over the past six months  funded the government with a continuing  resolution five times which is not  normal  now when it became clear that Congress  wasn't going to meet their September  30th deadline that's when we got the  first continuing resolution and that was  valid until December 8th then on  December 8th we got our second  continuing resolution or CR as the cool  kids call it this time it was just for a  few weeks so until December 22nd then on  December 22nd preventing a government  shutdown over Christmas we got our third  CR and I just want to pause for a moment  because that's a really important aspect  of these CRS because they are government  funding laws the consequences of them  not being signed is a government  shutdown which makes them must sign  legislation and because these are must  sign pieces of legislation members of  Congress attach all kinds of things to  these laws so that their pet policies  can hitchhike away into law to these  extra provisions oftentimes have no  debate and more frequently than you  might