ouldn't get through this  in time for the vote and I just I point  out and this is not your fault  because you have no control over this  but this process leaves a lot to be  desired and I you know I I the life of  me I don't know why we go from one  crisis to another crisis to another  crisis I mean the urgency now is that if  we don't do something quickly we're  going to shut the government down again  and I it's got to be a better way to do  this and one in which people actually  know what they're voting on and so  despite the fact that it was literally  impossible for anyone in Congress to  read it the Omnibus passed the committee  at about 1:00 a.m. and then 12 hours  later by 1:00 p.m. on March 22nd it  passed the House of Representatives 256  to 167 the vote wasn't even close and  then the omnibus went to the Senate  where it didn't pass until 12 21 a.m.  which  was longer than most of the senators  expected it to take and there was a  senator who complained publicly on the  Senate floor to Mitch McConnell's face  about the bill being passed at 12:20  a.m. but not because he didn't have a  chance to read it  oh no senator bob Corker of tennessee  was irritated because he was awoken from  his beauty sleep for the vote here's  what he said I had called down earlier  and asked the secretary if we're not  going to vote by 10 o'clock  if we could just vote at 8 o'clock in  the morning I mean this is ridiculous  it's juvenile this is a juvenile process  that we go through every time we do one  of these I would respectfully ask our  leader who's been dealing with a lot  today and I'm glad that he has the job  that he has and I don't and the  Secretary has the job that she has and I  don't but could you explain to us what  has occurred over the last 11 hours that  keeps us here voting on a bill that we  all know it's going to pass regardless  of how we vote on it that has kept us  from just going ahead and voting it  could you explain to the body just very  quickly w